    | **Femicides/year, homicide by gender, killings by police**            | 58 refs but mostly generic — the femicide and police killing data isalmost invisible | | **Food Security** | 6                | **Min wage vs. min wage *needed* for food, affordability ratio**      | Almost unused — this is a powerful cost-of-living lens                 | | **Poverty**       | 2                | Headcount at $3.65/day and $6.85/day                                  | Only 3 refs — no dedicated poverty explorer                 | ### Build: **The Price of Survival** (`/the-price-tag/` enhancement) You already have a `/the-price-tag/` page (318L). Your `food_security` data contains a **devastating comparison**: `min_wage_usd` vs. `min_wage_needed_usd` (what you actually need to afford adequate food), plus `food_cost_affordability_ratio`. This data can power a visceral, shareable tool: *"In Sudan, you'd need to work 14 hours/day at minimum wage just to eat. In Norway, 1.2 hours."* No other crisis platform frames hunger this way. ### Build: **Femicide & State Violence Explorer** Your `security` dimension has `femicides_per_year`, `killings_by_police`, and gendered homicide rates. This data exists for 200 countries and is **almost completely invisible**. A dedicated explorer (or a layer on the Sorrow Map) would be genuinely unique — nobody visualizes state violence and gender-based killings at this scale alongside hunger and conflict data. ### Build: **Prison & Justice Atlas** Your `justice` dimension has rule of law, prison overcrowding, pre-trial detention rates. Cross-reference with your Registry dossiers and Corruption Radar to create a "justice system health" view per country. --- ## 2. 📜 The Narrative Gap — Only 22 of 200 Countries Have Timelines ``` crisis_timelines.json: 22 countries with narrative history world_backbone.json:   200 countries in the data ``` **178 countries have rich numerical data but zero narrative.** A user visiting Sudan sees a devastating data profile — but visiting Botswana or Uruguay sees raw numbers with no story. ### Build: **Auto-Generated Country Narratives** Use the data backbone to algorithmically generate a narrative paragraph for each country. You already have the pieces: - The vulnerability scorer (`lib/vulnerability.ts`) produces composite scores - The campaign needs analyzer (`lib/campaign.ts`) identifies the most acute problems - Crosslink rules already generate contextual descriptions Wire these together to produce templated but data-accurate narratives: *"Uruguay's hunger rate (2.5%) is among the lowest globally, but its Gini coefficient of 41.7 reveals deep inequality. Child labor affects X thousand children..."* --- ## 3. 🔔 The Service Worker Has Skeletons for Features That Don't Exist Your `sw.js` (749L) documents these capabilities: ``` • Background sync queue for POST-like operations • Push notifications for crisis alerts ``` **Neither is wired to actual functionality.** The `push` event listener and `sync` event handler aren't registered. The infrastructure is documented but dormant. ### Build: **Web Push Subscription for Crisis Alerts** The alert engine (`lib/alert-engine.ts`) can evaluate rules and fire notifications. Wire it: - Users subscribe to push notifications from The Watch or The Alerts - A companion script (running on the data refresh CI/CD) checks for threshold breaches - The service worker receives the push and displays it — even when the browser is closed - This is **critical for the platform's mission** — "country X just crossed into famine" alerts reaching activists in real time ### Build: **Background Sync for Mesh Messages** The `queueWriteRequest` function in the SW captures POST requests. Wire it to the mesh store-and-forward system (`lib/mesh-store.ts`): when connectivity returns, the SW automatically flushes queued mesh messages, making offline coordination seamless. --- ## 4. 🎓 The Academy Has 4 Courses — But No Pathways or Community The Academy (`lib/education.ts`, 763L) has 4 courses: Global Hunger, Economics of War, Corruption, Climate Displacement. Each has modules, quizzes, and generates certificates. But: - **No learning pathways** — "If you care about X, take these 3 courses in order" - **No user-created content** — can't submit a course - **No discussion** — no way to discuss what you learned - **Only 4 topics** — no courses on digital security, nonviolent resistance tactics, mutual aid organizing ### Build: **Expand The Academy with Mission-Critical Courses** Add courses that directly serve the platform's purpose: - **Digital OpSec 101** (using The Mask, The Cipher, dead drops) - **Nonviolent Resistance Strategy** (leverage `lib/resistance.ts` — Chenoweth data) - **Citizen Journalism** (leverage `lib/citizen-tools.ts` — secure media capture) - **Mutual Aid Organizing** (using The Trail, The Web, Action Circles) Each course could cross-link to the relevant tools, creating a **learn → act** loop. --- ## 5. 🖨️ The Print Brief Is Powerful But Limited Your print feature (`app/print/[iso3]/PrintableBrief.tsx`, 528L) generates a **printable one-page country briefing** — a phenomenal feature for offline activism. But it's limited tocountries that have dossier data. ### Build: **Universal Printable Briefings + Protocol X Checklist Bundles** - Generate a printable brief for **all 200 countries** using the data backbone (not just the 5 with dossiers) - Create a **"print pack"** mode: select 5 countries → get a multi-page PDF-style printable bundle for distribution at events, meetings, or offline communities - Add a **Protocol X survival checklist generator**: pick your threat profile → get a printable, physical checklist of actions and supplies --- ## 6. ⚠️ No Error Reporting or Data Correction Path There is **no mechanism for users to flag incorrect data**. For a platform whose entire credibility rests on data accuracy, this is a significant gap. If a number is wrong, there's no "report this" button anywhere. ### Build: **Crowdsource Data Corrections** - Add a subtle `[ ⚠ FLAG ]` button on data points across the Sorrow Map and country detail pages - Users submit corrections with a source URL (stored locally, exportable as a signed correction package using your existing crypto infrastructure) - Corrections can be shared via The Web's mesh or submitted as pull-request-style packages - This creates a **community-verified data layer** on top of the authoritative backbone --- ## Summary: The Three Highest-Impact Builds   ────────────────────────────────────────   ▸ render_sankey autovisualiser     data: {"nodes": [{"name": "Auto-Narratives (178 countries)", "category": "gap"}, {"name": "Price of Survival Tool", "category": "gap"}, {"name": "Web Push Alerts", "category":... ### Across my three analyses, these rise to the top as the most impactful things to build: | #     | Build                                                               | Why It Matters                                                                            | What Already Exists                                                        | |-------|---------------------------------------------------------------------|-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|----------------------------------------------------------------------------| | **1** | **Auto-generated country narratives** (178 countries have no story) | A platform about empathy needs stories, not just numbers. 89% of countries are data-only. | `vulnerability.ts`, `campaign.ts`, crosslinks — all the pieces are there   | | **2** | **Web Push crisis alerts**                                          | SW infrastructure exists but is dormant. Real-time alerts = real-world impact.            | `sw.js` (push handlers documented), `alert-engine.ts`, `trigger-engine.ts` | | **3** | **Price of Survival tool**                                          | The min-wage-vs-food-needed data is devastating and uniquely framed. Viral potential.     | `food_security` dimension in backbone, `the-price-tag` page skeleton       | | **4** | **OpSec + Resistance Academy courses**                              | The learn→act loop is the platform's theory of change. Currently only 4 courses.          | `education.ts` framework, `resistance.ts`, `citizen-tools.ts`              | | **5** | **Correlation Explorer** (from my 2nd analysis)                     | 425L tested library, zero UI. Lowest-hanging fruit in the entire codebase.                | `correlation.ts` — fully built and tested                                  | --- **I've now mapped this codebase from three angles:** feature gaps, orphaned libraries, and now user-journey/content-depth gaps. The pattern across all three is the same — **V for X has built more infrastructure than it surfaces**. The highest-leverage work isn't building new engines; it's connecting existing ones to users. **Which of these should I start building?** I can implement any of them right now — full lib + page + tests + i18n, following your existing patterns.
